Online Tutorials and Textbooks

Learn JavaScript concepts through structured lessons and guides.





Eloquent JavaScript
https://eloquentjavascript.net/
A free, online textbook covering JavaScript basics to advanced topics like async programming. Perfect for deepening your understanding with clear explanations and exercises.
Why: Great for self-paced learning and reference, especially for Week 15’s async topics.



W3Schools JavaScript
https://www.w3schools.com/js/default.asp
Free, interactive tutorials on JavaScript basics, DOM manipulation, and APIs. Includes examples you can try in your browser.
Why: Ideal for quick reference and practicing Module 7’s Fetch API and JSON parsing.



MDN Web Docs: JavaScript
https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript
The go-to resource for JavaScript documentation, with beginner-friendly guides and in-depth references on topics like promises and async/await.
Why: Authoritative and great for understanding Week 15’s asynchronous concepts.



freeCodeCamp JavaScript Curriculum
https://www.freecodecamp.org/learn/javascript-algorithms-and-data-structures/
Free, interactive lessons covering JavaScript basics, functional programming, and APIs. Includes challenges to test your skills.
Why: Perfect for hands-on practice with coding exercises tied to course topics.



LinkedIn Learning: JavaScript Essential Training
https://www.linkedin.com/learning/javascript-essential-training/
A comprehensive course with video lessons on JavaScript, including DOM manipulation and APIs. Access provided through your school account.
Why: Structured video tutorials to reinforce Module 7 and Week 15 concepts. Log in with your school credentials.

Coding Projects

Build real-world projects to apply JavaScript skills, like the Advice Generator from Module 7.





Frontend Mentor: JavaScript Challenges
https://www.frontendmentor.io/challenges?type=free%2Cfree-plus
Free front-end projects with designs provided. Focus on adding JavaScript for interactivity (e.g., fetching data, updating the page).
Why: Practice integrating APIs and dynamic rendering, building on Module 7’s Fetch skills.



JavaScript30
https://javascript30.com
A free 30-day challenge with video tutorials to build 30 JavaScript projects. HTML and CSS are provided, so you focus on writing JavaScript.
Why: Fun, hands-on projects to practice DOM manipulation and async JavaScript (e.g., API calls).

Coding Challenges

Test your JavaScript skills with interactive problems and exercises.





SoloLearn JavaScript
https://www.sololearn.com/
Free, bite-sized JavaScript lessons and quizzes you can access on your phone, like Duolingo for programmers. Download the app to practice anywhere.
Why: Great for quick practice on the go, reinforcing loops, functions, and APIs.



Coddy.tech JavaScript Challenges
https://coddy.tech/explore/tag/javascript
Free JavaScript coding challenges ranging from beginner to advanced. Practice writing functions, handling data, and more.
Why: Builds problem-solving skills for Week 15’s async programming and Module 7’s JSON parsing.



Codewars
https://www.codewars.com/
Free coding challenges (“katas”) in JavaScript and other languages. Solve problems and compare your solutions with others.
Why: Sharpens logic and coding skills, with some challenges involving async tasks.

Coding Games

Learn JavaScript through fun, interactive games that teach coding concepts.





CodingGame
https://www.codingame.com/start/
Free coding games where you write JavaScript to solve puzzles and control game characters.
Why: Engaging way to practice logic and JavaScript syntax in a game environment.



CodeCombat
https://codecombat.com/
A free game where you write JavaScript to control a hero in a fantasy world. Great for beginners.
Why: Fun way to learn loops, conditionals, and functions interactively.



Untrusted
https://alexnisnevich.github.io/untrusted/
A free JavaScript game where you edit code to navigate levels and solve puzzles.
Why: Teaches problem-solving and JavaScript in a unique, engaging format.



Robocode
https://robocode.sourceforge.io/
A free game where you program a robot in JavaScript (or other languages) to battle others.
Why: Advanced but fun for applying logic and event-driven programming.



Elevator Saga
https://play.elevatorsaga.com/
A free game where you write JavaScript to control elevators and solve transport challenges.
Why: Great for practicing event handling and logic, similar to async tasks.

Tips for Using These Resources





Start Simple: Begin with W3Schools or SoloLearn for quick practice, then try Eloquent JavaScript for deeper learning.



Build Projects: Use JavaScript30 or Frontend Mentor to create projects like the Advice Generator, applying Fetch and async skills.



Practice Daily: Spend 10–15 minutes on SoloLearn or Codewars to reinforce concepts.



Experiment: Try coding games to make learning fun and test your skills in new ways.



Use School Resources: Log into LinkedIn Learning with your school account for guided video lessons.



Check Progress: Use MDN Web Docs to understand tricky topics like promises or async/await from Week 15.